module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.doctrine_safety_claim_stated_wider_than_the_census_that_delivers_it

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data doctrine_safety_claim_stated_wider_than_the_census_that_delivers_it: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "doctrine_safety_claim_stated_wider_than_the_census_that_delivers_it" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a DOCTRINE in the design authority states a safety property unconditionally while the mechanism that would deliver it ranges, BY SIGNED RULING, over a declared subset -- so a reader correctly following the doctrine infers a census the ruling deliberately does not provide** (INVALID STATE: a normative sentence of the form `in a fail-closed substrate X is guaranteed` stands in the document a reader consults FIRST, with no scope qualifier, while the executing mechanism that produces X admits its subjects by a roster an operator signed with a stated price. NOTHING IS HIDDEN AND NOTHING IS BROKEN -- the limitation is declared where it is enforced, counted, written to the disposition artifact and announced per run. THE DEFECT IS ONLY THE JOIN: the two facts live in two documents, one of which is prescriptive and silent about the other. HARM: the doctrine instructs an author to RELY on the mechanism instead of doing the work the mechanism does, so a product-layer migration is performed with no census at all and the author has followed the authority exactly. This is DESIGN section 4b(1) rung inflation applied to a doctrine rather than to an error class, and it is worse in the same way: an inflated doctrine never ranks for climbing, because the document says the guarantee is already there.)",

    "THE RULING IS NOT A HOLE AND THIS ROW DOES NOT TREAT IT AS ONE, which is the correction that produced this row's final shape. `v2.workflow.required_floor` states it directly above `required_gate_prefixes`: `THE REQUIRED GATE: A STATIC ROSTER, BY OPERATOR RULING (2026-08-29, THE CI BANKRUPTCY)`, priced on the last green whole-corpus run -- 11,996 witnesses folded, 27 minutes preparing 4,171 modules on one core, 32 more to fold, 87 wall minutes, against ~50 squash-merges a day and fifteen consecutive red runs on main from two integration collisions no PR could have seen. `the required gate is the compiler floor and nothing else; the rest of the corpus runs as receipts, not as merge conditions`, and `this roster is a constant the operator signed`. It is a declared rung drop, `gunbc.rung_drop` `required_gate_bankruptcy`, whose restoration trigger is the derived selection that replaces the constant. Exclusions are recorded per identity -- counted, written to the disposition TSV, reported in the announcement, never silently skipped -- and roster nonmembership alone does not determine which decline an identity carries: `required_floor_site_disposition` decides long-home, fixture-home and cost-debt declines before it consults the roster. So the coverage loss is DECLARED POLICY WITH A STATED PRICE. Widening the roster is not this row's remedy, is not proposed here, and re-tendering that trade uninvited would be re-litigating a signed ruling on worse evidence than it was made on.",

    "THE MEASURED INSTANCE, joined at identity and at a named head. DESIGN section 3's replacement-migration standing rule says of delete-first that `in a fail-closed substrate the deletion is the census -- every real dependent refuses loudly, so what breaks is exactly what was load-bearing`. gunbc#10994 renamed `unsized` to `undispatchable` on `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_program_view` `RemainingShape`. FIVE consumer sites in three modules kept the old spelling -- `test.claim.instrument_sandbox_witness_test` at two constructor sites, `test.claim.instrument_tuner_witness_test` at one, `test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_program_view_witness_test` at one constructor site and at one stale field READ in `witness_fixture_shape_exact_populations` -- and from that head onward those three modules could not resolve. At head 46b4ba130cd, where the declaration already reads `undispatchable` and the consumers still read `unsized`, run 34689263434 reported `required-witnesses-floor` SUCCESS (job 103541547386) and `required-witnesses-build` SUCCESS (job 103541547478). The five sites were found by a lane reading review 64526, not by any red, and repaired in commit 6d6a39fbbcc.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT PROVES THIS IS COVERAGE AND NOT A WEAK TYPECHECKER, and it is the fact that makes the row a doctrine defect rather than a compiler defect: restoring the old spelling at one site yields `field 'unsized' not found in type 'RemainingShape'` together with `missing required field 'undispatchable'`. The substrate DOES refuse loudly. It simply never looked. None of the three module paths carries any of the 31 prefixes in `required_gate_prefixes`, every one of which is compiler-oriented -- `test.claim.payload_arm_excess_field_admission_test`'s own annotation states the intent as admitting `only compiler-oriented prefixes`, and the ruling names what is out: `nothing here is a product, fleet, deploy, secret, dashboard or roadmap witness`. `the deletion is the census` therefore holds for dependents in a run's prepared closure and did not hold for these three on the named runs; a product-layer dependent -- roadmap, instrument, harness, spark, fabric, belt -- is covered only when some seed of that run reaches it, which no roster entry does.",

    "THE SECOND HEAD, AND WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT ADD (receipt from eager-gull-33, authorised by bright-boar-435, 2026-09-12; every fact below re-verified here against the named commits rather than relayed). The same unrepaired state stands on a SECOND branch: gunbc#11014 at head 3b6f6c64b99 (`session/vivid-ant-470`) carries `undispatchable: Int` on the declaration and the same five old-spelling references in the same three modules, the same 4 constructor sites plus the field read `lv.remaining.unsized` in `witness_fixture_shape_exact_populations`. That branch shares no lineage with `session/eager-gull-33` -- their merge base is main at 8bc6ec1e0d8 -- and the break is also present at an earlier head of the first branch, b884668e8e2, whose run 34680031177 was likewise green. WHAT THIS IS NOT, and the distinction is worth the sentence because the obvious reading overstates it: this is NOT the rename being authored twice by two uncoordinated lanes. Both branches CONTAIN THE SAME RENAME COMMIT, 7e17bff1d27, so there is one authorship event carried onto two branches. What the second branch adds is a head where the floor REFUSED and the break was still not surfaced. The run on 3b6f6c64b99, 34700086950, concluded failure, and its sole blocker was `v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe.affected_set_universe_gate_processes_match_declared_gates` crossing its CPU budget after reaching its verdict -- the crossing at unchanged eval_steps that the owner of `v2.workflow.required_floor` ruled environmental -- with `blockers=1`. Its `required-floor-disposition` artifact carries all fourteen identities of the three consumer modules at `declined_outside_gate_closure` / `not_executed`. So a verdict existed for the author to read, and the break was not in it: a run's conclusion states the verdict, and only its blockers and dispositions state what the verdict covered. The measured greens over the broken state are the two heads of the first branch, b884668e8e2 (run 34680031177, disposition artifact not read in this row) and 46b4ba130cd (run 34689263434).",

    "THE COUNT IS FIVE REFERENCES AND NOT FIVE CONSTRUCTOR SITES, and the distinction is recorded because it was gotten wrong once by a careful measurer and would mislead any later reader. bright-boar-435 first measured four by grepping `unsized:` WITH THE COLON, which matches only record-literal fields and silently misses `lv.remaining.unsized`; the count was corrected by line number. The two are not interchangeable: repairing four constructors and stopping leaves the read unresolved, and the compiler reports them as different errors -- a missing required field at the literal versus an unknown field at the access. A pattern chosen for one syntactic form is not a population over the semantic one, and that miscount landed inside the receipt of a row whose whole subject is a denominator read wider than the mechanism that produced it.",

    "WHAT A GREEN FLOOR ESTABLISHES IS STATED PER DISPOSITION AND NEVER FOR THE RUN AS A WHOLE, because the run publishes three different facts about a declared identity and a green verdict is a statement about only one of them. PREPARATION is a closure: the modules reachable from `required_floor_runner` `closure_seeds` -- the required-gate prefixes, the floor runtime-authority modules, the modules containing selected changed test identities, and the wet-schedule entries -- were compiled, so a non-resolving reference inside that closure refuses. SELECTION is per identity: an unexecuted identity retains the specific decline the owning mechanism assigned. In this row's specimens `declined_outside_required_gate` records a prepared identity excluded by ordinary gate selection and `declined_outside_gate_closure` records exclusion before preparation; other decline reasons, such as cost debt, remain distinct -- run 34700086950 carries `test.claim.roadmap_authority.witness_altered_copy_of_declared_node_is_counted` at `declined_cost_debt` beside an identity of the same module at `planned_as_changed_witness`. A PASS is established only for an identity whose published terminal outcome is `passed`. A green therefore does not establish that every assertion in a prepared module passed, and it does not establish that an identity outside the prepared closure resolves. Which identities fell where is read from the `required-floor-disposition` artifact of the named run; a green floor log carries totals and non-passing lines, so a passed identity and an unselected one are both absent from it and cannot be told apart there.",

    "THE MEASURED EXCLUSION, SCOPED TO THE NAMED RUN. On run 34689263434, the green run over head 46b4ba130cd, the `required-floor-disposition` artifact carries every identity of the three consumer modules at `declined_outside_gate_closure` with `outcome=not_executed`: the one identity of `test.claim.instrument_sandbox_witness`, the one of `test.claim.instrument_tuner_witness`, and the twelve of `test.claim.roadmap_program_view_witness`, against that run's summary `total=18592 planned=3770 declined_outside_gate_closure=13522`. No seed of that run reached those modules, so they were not compiled and their non-resolving references could not refuse. This row claims that for those modules on that run and for the other named heads where the same state was measured. It does not claim that every unchanged product-layer consumer is outside every prepared closure: a consumer reachable from another seed is prepared whether or not its author edited it, and whether a particular consumer is covered is a question answered by that run's artifact, not by this sentence.",

    "THE STRUCTURAL CONTROL. gunbc#11162 at ff6c853af9b added `import std.token_count { token_count }` to `dag/test/claim/spark/spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test.dag` for a module that does not exist, and changed test identities in that module. The required floor refused it: run 34697340022, job 103562976623, `unresolved import: module 'std.token_count' not found (imported by 'test.claim.spark.spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test')`, `floor_class=structural`, `blockers=1`, job failure. `test.claim.spark.` matches none of the 31 prefixes, as none of this row's three modules do, so roster membership does not separate this refusal from the missed rename. What this control shows is that a module in the prepared closure -- here reached as the home of selected changed identities -- is compiled and refuses a non-resolving reference; it is not evidence about which seed a given unchanged consumer would or would not reach.",

    "THE EXECUTION CONTROL, WITH ITS OUTCOMES RECORDED AS WHAT WAS MEASURED. On gunbc#11162 at 7514dd45ad594172ab9fbe12849719f8b248d14e, run 34699476983, floor job 103571288591 reported `claims_failed=4`. Three of the failures are `test.claim.spark.spark_first_party_serving_witness_test.every_use_program_is_not_engaged_and_names_its_basis`, `a_non_first_party_use_refuses_as_unsupported` and `a_superseded_posture_makes_the_first_party_sanction_stale`, each carried at `disposition=planned_as_changed_witness` and `outcome=failed`, each returning `Bool(false)`. They were planned because their own identities were changed, compiled, evaluated, and FAILED; no receipt here states that they passed. What this control shows is that selected identities in a non-roster module receive semantic evaluation, not only structural refusal.",

    "A SEPARATE HAZARD, NOT MEASURED AT ANY HEAD, AND LABELED AS SUCH. The author of those witnesses described, from reading the source, a counterexample the executed failures do not exhibit: after that change nothing admits a permit, so the first witness's permit arm is unreachable, and the other two feed production route facts to a decision whose telemetry is unread, so a route-facts refusal fires before the refusal they name -- and a witness written to accept any refusal would pass on the wrong one. That is a source-derived statement of what an unselected or differently written assertion could do. It is not an executed result, it is not the outcome at 7514dd45a, and the wrong-refusal-pass class it describes belongs with [[discriminating_arm_built_but_never_enrolled]] and [[executed_conjunct_discriminates_nothing]] rather than with this row.",

    "SELECTION IS PER IDENTITY, NOT PER FILE, AND PREPARATION AND EXECUTION ARE SEPARATE. On run 34704198398 the module `test.claim.spark.spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test` carries nine identities: eight at `planned_as_changed_witness` / `passed`, and `an_observed_route_binds_and_the_offer_carries_it` at `declined_outside_required_gate` / `not_executed`. The module was prepared, so a non-resolving reference anywhere in it would have refused, while the one unchanged identity's assertion was not evaluated. Editing a file therefore neither guarantees that its other assertions run nor is the only route by which a module is compiled.",

    "THE PLAUSIBLE WRONG READING, RECORDED BECAUSE TWO CAREFUL PEOPLE REACHED IT FROM THE SAME TWO DATA POINTS AND A THIRD WOULD TOO. Set the structural control beside the missed rename and the obvious story is DECLARATION GRAIN: a green refuses an unresolved module IMPORT and says nothing about whether every NAME resolves, so imports are walled and declaration references are not. It fits both specimens and it is not what the disposition artifacts show: on run 34689263434 the missed consumers were `declined_outside_gate_closure`, not prepared, so no error of any kind in them could refuse on that run, and on the structural control the refusing module was prepared. The reading sends a reader hunting a typechecker gap that does not exist, and it implies that a broken import in any unedited consumer would refuse, when whether that consumer is compiled depends on whether some seed of the run reaches it. It was settled by the published `required-floor-disposition` artifact together with `required_gate_prefixes` and `closure_seeds` in source, not by run outcomes. THE STANDING LESSON, and it is this row's own subject turned on its own investigation: a mechanism inferred from which runs happened to redden is a story fitted to outcomes, and the outcomes of a SELECTED population cannot distinguish what the selector excluded from what a checker tolerated; the disposition artifact is where that distinction is published.",

    "TWO MECHANISM FACTS A READER NEEDS, because together they are why the author of the rename could not have been warned. FIRST: CHANGING AN AUTHORITY DOES NOT ENROL ITS UNCHANGED WITNESS CONSUMERS. `required_floor_runner` `closure_seeds` seeds preparation from the required-gate prefixes, the floor's runtime-authority modules, the modules containing SELECTED CHANGED test identities (`edits.edited_test_fns`), and the wet-schedule entries. Editing a type does not by that edit prepare a witness that merely uses it; such a witness is compiled only if some other seed reaches it. SECOND: DISCOVERY AND PREPARATION ARE DIFFERENT RANGES. The runner discovers declarations corpus-wide but prepares strictly, and `DeclinedOutsideGateClosure` (discovered, NOT prepared) is a distinct disposition from `DeclinedOutsideRequiredGate` (prepared, not executed). So EVERY DISCOVERED IDENTITY RECEIVED A DISPOSITION is not EVERY DEPENDENT WAS CHECKED: a complete-looking identity census coexists with incomplete semantic checking, and that is the sentence a reader most needs and the one no artifact says.",

    "THE RECOGNITION RULE, phrased so it fires on prose and not on code: for every unconditional safety sentence in an authority, NAME THE EXECUTING MECHANISM THAT PRODUCES IT AND THEN NAME ITS SUBJECT. If the mechanism admits its subject by an authored roster, prefix list, allowlist, or any membership test that is not the whole corpus, the sentence is scoped and the authority must carry the scope -- even, and especially, when the narrowing is a signed ruling, because a declared limitation is exactly the kind a reader assumes someone else already reconciled with the doctrine. The tell is a sentence whose subject noun is the SUBSTRATE (`in a fail-closed substrate`, `every real dependent`, `the compiler refuses`) beside a mechanism whose subject is a SELECTION. A doctrine may describe a property of the language; it may not describe a property of the GATE as though it were a property of the language.",

    "WHY THE REPAIR IS THE WORDING AND NOT THE ROSTER, which is the part a reader is most likely to get backwards. The invalid state is the UNQUALIFIED SENTENCE, and it is invalid whatever the gate's width: a gate that admitted the whole corpus today would still leave the doctrine asserting a property of the substrate rather than of a mechanism, and the next cost ruling that narrows the gate -- the 2026-08-29 ruling being the receipt that such rulings happen -- would silently re-open it. The honest repair scopes the claim to the census the gate actually performs and names what a product-layer migration must do instead, which is to enumerate its consumers by hand before deleting the root.",

    "BOUNDARY AGAINST [[witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red]], WHICH OWNS THE MECHANISM AND IS NOT THIS CLASS, and the distinction is the whole reason a second identity is minted. That row's second specimen is structurally the same event -- gunbc#10966 added five required fields to `extdeps.github.workflow_runs` `WorkflowRun` and four literals in a gate-excluded claim file stopped compiling with the floor green -- and its widened trigger (compile every claim-root file AND every construction site of a type whose declared field set changed, regardless of gate closure) would have caught the five `RemainingShape` sites too. So those sites are a further specimen of THAT row's population and no new mechanism is claimed for them here. What is new is that the DESIGN AUTHORITY tells an author the opposite: that row's subject is a witness file that is silently absent, and its repair is a compile step; this row's subject is a normative sentence, and its repair is words in `gunbc.design_document`. Landing that row's trigger in full leaves the doctrine's wording exactly as unqualified, and correcting the wording executes nothing -- disjoint repairs, which is the test that settles it.",

    "BOUNDARY AGAINST [[required_lane_green_over_a_population_it_never_offered]], WHICH IS THE NEAREST DENOMINATOR ROW. There the defect is that a terminal verdict is built from the offered subset and does not disclose the identities it declined to offer, and the repair is a total disposition over the declared universe. That repair is about the RUN'S REPORT. The present class survives it untouched: a floor verdict naming every declined identity would still be read beside a design sentence promising that deletion is a census, and the author of a product-layer rename does not read floor verdicts before renaming a field -- they read the doctrine. Conversely, correcting the doctrine leaves every verdict exactly as it is. Same excluded population, different artifact, disjoint repairs.",

    "BOUNDARY AGAINST [[stale_present_tense_coverage_claim_in_the_authority_consulted_first]], WHICH SHARES THE LOCATION AND NOT THE HISTORY. There a sentence is written AT A DE-ENROLMENT to discharge a reviewer's objection -- `enforcement is NOT reduced: it executes per-PR via X` -- and it becomes false because X is on no executing path; the sentence was once a live compensation and the class is its decay. Here nothing decayed: the doctrine was stated at substrate grain from the beginning, and the ruling that narrowed the gate landed later and elsewhere without touching it. A staleness detector re-checking every coverage sentence against today's executing paths would not fire, because the sentence names no consumer to check. What is missing is a SCOPE, not a refresh.",

    "BOUNDARY AGAINST [[check_subject_narrower_than_its_declared_claim]], which is the same asymmetry one layer down. There a CHECK is cited for a population wider than the one its implementation ranges over, and the repair is to widen the check or narrow its citation. Here there is no defective check to widen: the substrate's refusal is total and correct at every site it reaches, and the floor's selection is a priced ruling rather than an accident of first implementation -- which is precisely the distinction that row makes load-bearing when it says the narrowing `is never a decision`. Here it WAS a decision. The narrowness lives only in the JOIN between a doctrine's stated subject and a mechanism's signed subject, which is why the population is prose in `gunbc.design_document` rather than any executing declaration.",

    "THE POPULATION IS NAMED BY ITS PRODUCER AND NOT TRANSCRIBED, because a count of unqualified safety sentences copied here would rot beside the document it describes. The candidates are the sentences of `gunbc.design_document` that assert a guarantee in the present tense over a substrate-grain subject; the mechanisms they could rest on are the required lanes rostered at `gunbc.witness_floor_workflow` `required_lanes_roster` and the prefix roster at `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_gate_prefixes`, read beside the standing drops at `gunbc.rung_drop`. `the deletion is the census` is the one adjudicated. No number is recorded here and none may be quoted from this row.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable, and the mitigation is that a reader happened to read a review. Nothing holds this class today: the doctrine is prose, no `Accepted` program can read it (DESIGN section 4c), and the one instrument that could have contradicted it -- the required floor -- reported SUCCESS on the named runs while the modules where the doctrine failed sat outside that run's prepared closure. CEILING: 2, mechanically preventable, and the ceiling is honestly 2 and not higher because the subject is an authored English sentence whose intended scope is not derivable from any modeled fact; what IS derivable is the gate's admitted subject, so a doctrine sentence can be REQUIRED to cite the mechanism it rests on and that citation can be joined against the mechanism's actual subject. Making the unqualified sentence unwritable would need the doctrine itself modeled as a claim with a declared subject, which is not a capability this repo has or has planned.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, STATED AS A CAPABILITY AND NOT AS AN ARTIFACT: a design-document guarantee sentence carries a DECLARED MECHANISM REFERENCE and a DECLARED SUBJECT, and a check joins that subject against the mechanism's own admitted subject and refuses, typed and located, when the declared subject is wider. SUFFICIENT FOR: every unconditional guarantee sentence in the authority, not only this one. Editing the wording of `the deletion is the census` repairs the single instance and fires NOTHING -- the next doctrine written at substrate grain over a rostered mechanism is exactly as dark, which is why the wording repair is not offered here as the trigger.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
